<h3>The <code>ollama launch</code> Command</h3>
<p><code>ollama launch</code> starts a coding agent in one command, with Ollama as the model backend:</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">ollama launch claude
ollama launch codex-app
ollama launch opencode
ollama launch openclaw
</code></pre>
<p><code>ollama launch</code> differs from <code>ollama run</code> in scope. <code>ollama run</code> opens a chat with a single model. <code>ollama launch</code> starts an agent such as Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode (an open-source coding agent) or OpenClaw (an open-source personal AI assistant) on top of the model. Both commands work with local and cloud models.</p>
<h2>Ollama System Requirements</h2>
<p>Ollama&#39;s hardware requirements vary significantly based on the model you run and its precision.</p>
<h3>Model Parameters</h3>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Parameters</strong>: numerical values a model learns during training.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A model&#39;s name states its parameter count (7B, 13B, 70B). More parameters usually mean more capable responses and higher memory requirements. All parameters must load into VRAM or system RAM when a model runs.</p>
<h3>Choosing a Precision: FP16, Q8 or Q4</h3>
<p>Most default Ollama tags, like <code>llama3.1:8b</code>, are 4-bit (Q4_K_M) builds sized to fit laptops and 8GB GPUs. This 4-bit default favors convenience over quality.</p>
<p>Q8 is the best balance for most Ollama workloads. <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2411.02355v4" target="_blank" rel="noopener">8-bit quantization</a> halves memory use with minimal quality loss compared with full precision. FP16 delivers full quality and fits cases where outputs must match the original model, such as evaluation baselines or fine-tune comparisons.</p>
<p>Q4 cuts memory to roughly a third of FP16, but quality loss grows on <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2504.04823v1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">smaller models, reasoning-heavy work</a>, and <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2505.20276v3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">long-context tasks</a>. Studies show the largest quality drops in those cases. Use Q4 only when nothing else fits.</p>
<p>To choose a precision, run a model&#39;s full tag instead of the default. Each Ollama model page lists available builds in its Tags tab:</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">ollama run llama3.1:8b-instruct-q8_0    # 8-bit
ollama run llama3.1:8b-instruct-fp16    # full precision
</code></pre>
<h3>VRAM Requirements by Model Size</h3>
<p>FP16 needs about 2GB of VRAM per 1B parameters, and Q8 needs about 1GB. Add 10-20% for the context window (KV cache). Q4 needs roughly half the Q8 figure.</p>
<div class="Post-module__zd2qRG__tableScroll"><table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="text-align:left;">Model Size</th>
      <th style="text-align:left;">Example Tags</th>
      <th style="text-align:center;">FP16 VRAM</th>
      <th style="text-align:center;">Q8 VRAM</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>3B-4B</td>
      <td>llama3.2:3b, qwen3:4b</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">7-10GB</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">4-6GB</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>7B-8B</td>
      <td>llama3.1:8b, qwen3:8b</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">16-19GB</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">9-11GB</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>12B-14B</td>
      <td>gemma3:12b, qwen3:14b</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">27-33GB</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">14-18GB</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>27B-32B</td>
      <td>gemma3:27b, qwen3:32b</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">60-75GB</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">30-38GB</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>70B</td>
      <td>llama3.3:70b</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">145-160GB</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">75-85GB</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>

<h3>Why VRAM Is King for AI</h3>
<p><a href="https://intuitionlabs.ai/articles/hbm-vs-ddr-memory-comparison" target="_blank" rel="noopener">System RAM transfers data at 20-70GB/s, whereas VRAM moves data at 350-4,800GB/s.</a> That bandwidth gap makes GPU inference much faster than CPU inference.</p>
<p>When a model exceeds VRAM, Ollama offloads the remaining layers to system RAM. Offloading prevents a crash but sharply cuts tokens/second.</p>
<p>Run <code>ollama ps</code> while a model is loaded to check its placement. A reading of 100% GPU means the whole model fits in VRAM.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Compare GPUs by workload and budget in the <a href="/blog/best-gpu-for-llm">Thunder Compute guide to the best GPU for LLM work</a>.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>How Much Disk Space Does Ollama Need?</h3>
<p>The Ollama installer is small: about 190 MB on macOS and 1.5GB on Windows. Models take far more space. An 8B model needs about 5GB at Q4, 8.5GB at Q8 and <a href="https://ollama.com/library/llama3.1:8b-instruct-fp16" target="_blank" rel="noopener">16GB at FP16</a>. 70B models range from <a href="https://computingforgeeks.com/ollama-models-cheat-sheet/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">about 43GB at Q4</a> to over 140GB at FP16.</p>
