<h2>Use Cases and Applications</h2>
<p>Ollama supports development, business and education use cases.</p>
<p><strong>Development:</strong> Developers use Ollama for code completion, programming assistance, document analysis and research on proprietary datasets. Local models carry no API costs or rate limits.</p>
<p><strong>Business Applications:</strong> Regulated industries benefit most from local deployment, because Ollama keeps inference on infrastructure they control. Customer service chatbots, internal knowledge-base search, content generation and legal document analysis all run without exposing data to third-party servers.</p>
<p><strong>Education:</strong> Schools and universities can use Ollama to avoid ongoing cloud costs for teaching AI concepts, student projects and academic research.</p>
<h2>Why Run Ollama in the Cloud?</h2>
<p>Cloud GPUs remove the trade-off between model size and quality that local hardware forces. An 8GB GPU runs an 8B model at Q4, but not a 32B model at Q8 or an 8B model at FP16. Closing that gap with owned hardware is expensive: <a href="https://www.thundercompute.com/blog/nvidia-h100-pricing" target="_blank">an NVIDIA H100 runs from around $25K</a> and <a href="https://www.thundercompute.com/blog/nvidia-rtx-pro-6000-pricing" target="_blank">a dedicated RTX PRO 6000 around $13.25K</a> as of June 2026.</p>
<p>Thunder Compute offers a pre-configured Ollama template on the RTX A6000, NVIDIA A100 and NVIDIA H100, billed per minute with no upfront cost. A 48GB RTX A6000 runs a 32B model at Q8 or a 14B model at FP16. Prompts stay on an instance you control instead of going to a third-party API.</p>
<h2>Step-by-Step: Running Ollama on Thunder Compute</h2>
<p>Thunder Compute&#39;s one-click Ollama template installs NVIDIA drivers and CUDA automatically, with nothing to configure.</p>
<h3>Step 1: Create a GPU Instance</h3>
<p>Create a Thunder Compute instance with this configuration:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>GPU:</strong> NVIDIA RTX A6000*</li>
<li><strong>Size:</strong> 6 vCPUs (48GB RAM)*</li>
<li><strong>Template:</strong> Ollama</li>
<li><strong>Disk:</strong> 100GB</li>
</ul>
<p><em>*Adjust GPU and disk based on the model and precision you plan to run.</em></p>
<p>The Ollama template ships with Ubuntu 22.04, NVIDIA drivers and the Ollama binary.</p>
<h3>Step 2: Connect</h3>
<p>Connect to the live instance with the <a href="https://www.thundercompute.com/docs/vscode/quickstart" target="_blank">Thunder Compute extension for VS Code or Cursor</a>, or with your OS&#39;s CLI.</p>
<h3>Step 3: Start Ollama</h3>
<p>Run <code>start-ollama</code> to launch Open WebUI. The command returns a link to the Open WebUI interface.</p>
<h3>Step 4: Add a Model</h3>
<p>The Ollama template ships with no models pre-installed.</p>
<ul>
<li>Head over to the <a href="https://ollama.com/search" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Ollama Models</a> page.</li>
<li>Select a model.</li>
<li>Go back to Open WebUI.</li>
<li>Click <strong>Select a model</strong>.</li>
<li>Type the model name to begin the download.</li>
</ul>
<p>Download time depends on model size.</p>
<h3>Step 5: Start Chatting</h3>
<p>The model is now live. Send it a complex prompt, ask it to write a Python script or use it to analyze a dataset.</p>
<h2>Last Thoughts on Ollama</h2>
<p>Ollama runs capable open models on your own machine for free, with an optional cloud tier for larger models. Start locally for full control over your data and models. When your hardware runs out, <a href="https://www.thundercompute.com/products/instances">launch the Ollama template on Thunder Compute</a>. A 48GB RTX A6000 costs $0.35/hr, billed by the minute.</p>
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<details style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
<summary style="display: list-item; cursor: pointer;"><h3 style="display: inline;">Is Ollama free?</h3></summary>

<p>Yes. Ollama is free, open source and unlimited on your own hardware. Ollama Cloud has a free tier, then Pro ($20/mo), Max ($100/mo) and Team ($500/mo) plans with monthly usage credits. Thunder Compute rents a 48GB RTX A6000 for $0.35/hr.</p>
</details>

<details style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
<summary style="display: list-item; cursor: pointer;"><h3 style="display: inline;">What is Ollama Cloud?</h3></summary>

<p>Ollama Cloud runs models on Ollama&#39;s servers instead of your GPU, through the same CLI and API as local Ollama. Cloud models use a :cloud tag suffix, such as kimi-k2.6:cloud. Run ollama signin once; every request needs an internet connection.</p>
</details>

<details style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
<summary style="display: list-item; cursor: pointer;"><h3 style="display: inline;">Is Ollama safe to use?</h3></summary>

<p>Yes, when installed from ollama.com or the official GitHub releases. Its API listens only on localhost by default and has no built-in authentication. Never expose port 11434 to the internet without a reverse proxy, VPN or SSH tunnel.</p>
</details>

<details style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
<summary style="display: list-item; cursor: pointer;"><h3 style="display: inline;">What hardware do I need to run Ollama?</h3></summary>

<p>You need a GPU with enough VRAM for your model&#39;s precision: about 2GB per 1B parameters at FP16 and 1GB at Q8, plus context. An 8B model at Q8 fits a 12GB GPU. A 32B model at Q8 needs 30-38GB, usually a 48GB cloud GPU.</p>
</details>

<details style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
<summary style="display: list-item; cursor: pointer;"><h3 style="display: inline;">Can I use Ollama without a GPU?</h3></summary>

<p>Yes. Ollama supports CPU-only execution, but generation is much slower than on a GPU, especially for models above 7B. Use a dedicated GPU for anything beyond light testing.</p>
</details>

<details style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
<summary style="display: list-item; cursor: pointer;"><h3 style="display: inline;">When should I move from local Ollama to cloud infrastructure?</h3></summary>

<p>Move to the cloud when local hardware can&#39;t fit the model or precision you need. Cloud GPUs also suit consistent production performance and shared team access to GPU resources.</p>
</details></div></div></article></div></section></div><footer class="Footer-module__OJmioW__footer"><div class="padding-global border Footer-module__OJmioW__cover"><div class="container-large border-small Footer-module__OJmioW__top"><nav class="Footer-module__OJmioW__columns" aria-label="Footer"><div class="Footer-module__OJmioW__column"><h2 class="Footer-module__OJmioW__heading">Products</h2><ul class="Footer-module__OJmioW__list"><li><a class="Footer-module__OJmioW__link" href="/products/instances">Instances</a></li><li><a class="Footer-module__OJmioW__link" href="/products/sandboxes">Sandboxes</a></li><li><a class="Footer-module__OJmioW__link" href="/pricing">Pricing</a></li></ul></div><div class="Footer-module__OJmioW__column"><h2 class="Footer-module__OJmioW__heading">Developers</h2><ul class="Footer-module__OJmioW__list"><li><a href="https://www.thundercompute.com/docs" class="Footer-module__OJmioW__link">Documentation</a></li><li><a href="https://www.thundercompute.com/docs/cli/quickstart" class="Footer-module__OJmioW__link">CLI</a></li><li><a href="https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=ThunderCompute.thunder-compute" class="Footer-module__OJmioW__link">VS Code extension</a></li><li><a href="https://www.thundercompute.com/docs/mcp-server/quickstart" class="Footer-module__OJmioW__link">MCP server</a></li><li><a href="https://www.thundercompute.com/docs/guides/using-instance-templates" class="Footer-module__OJmioW__link">Templates</a></li><li><a href="https://www.thundercompute.com/docs/api-reference/instances/list-instances" class="Footer-module__OJmioW__link">REST API</a></li></ul></div><div class="Footer-module__OJmioW__column"><h2 class="Footer-module__OJmioW__heading">Resources</h2><ul class="Footer-module__OJmioW__list"><li><a class="Footer-module__OJmioW__link" aria-current="page" href="/blog">Blog</a></li><li><a href="https://www.thundercompute.com/docs/troubleshooting" class="Footer-module__OJmioW__link">Help center</a></li><li><a href="h